<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec_ohm" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
	<title>Linear Resistors</title>
			
	<corollary xml:id="vid-ohm">
	<title>Ohm's Law</title>
	
	<statement>
		<p>
			
		</p>

		<video youtube="HupkKuWKn1Y"><description>Describing linear resistors.</description></video>

	</statement>
  </corollary>

  <subsubsection><title>Key Ideas</title></subsubsection>
	
	<assumption>
		<title>Linear Resistor</title>
		<statement>
			<p>
				
			</p>
			<p>A <term>linear resistor</term> or Ohmic resistor has a resistance <m>R</m> that is <alert>constant</alert> (it is the same no matter what current is flowing through it).</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<p>
						<alert>The resistance can be calculated from material properties.</alert>  A resistor with length <m>L</m>, cross-sectional area <m>A</m>, and resistitivty <m>\rho</m> has resistance <me>R = \rho \frac{L}{A}</me>
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						<alert>Resistivity depends on temperature.</alert>  In general, an object will only be a good linear resistor in a regime where its temperature is not changing substantially.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						<alert>Current flows through a resistor from high voltage to low voltage.</alert>  This is also true for nonlinear resistors, but it is <alert>not necessarily true</alert> for elements like batteries.
					</p>
				</li>
			</ul>
		<figure xml:id="CD_R">
		<title>Ohmic Resistor Diagram</title>
		<caption>The circuit diagram representation for a linear resistor.</caption>
		<image source="CD_R.png" width = "10%" ><description>A jagged line representing a linear resistor.</description></image>
	</figure>
		</statement>
	</assumption>

	<principle>
		<title>Ohm's Law</title>
		<statement>
			<p>
				
			</p>
			<p>
				<term>Ohm's Law</term> relates the voltage <m>V</m> across a circuit element to the current <m>I</m> flowing through that element: <me>V = IR</me>
			</p>
			<p>
				<alert><m>R</m> is the resistance of the circuit element.</alert>  For a linear resistor, the resistance is constant.  For nonlinear resistors like light bulbs, the resistance will be a non-constant function of <m>I</m>.
			</p>
		</statement>
	</principle>

	
	<subsubsection>
	<title>Activities</title>
	<p>Shown below is a circuit with three resistors.</p>
			<figure xml:id="circuit1">
			  <title>Circuit Diagram</title>
				<caption>A circuit diagram consisting of a battery and three resistors. Resistors 1 and 2 are in series, and are both in parallel with Resistor 3.</caption>
				<image source="circ/circuit-0.png" width = "50%" ><description>A circuit diagram consisting of a battery and three resistors. Resistors 1 and 2 are in series, and are both in parallel with Resistor 3.</description></image>
			</figure>
		<investigation>
      <title>Current Prediction</title>
      <p></p>
			
			<p>Assume that <m>R_1 \gt R_2</m>. Predict whether the current through resistor 2 is greater than, less than, or equal to the current through resistor 1. Explain your answer.</p>
		</investigation>
		<investigation>
      <title>Voltage Prediction</title>
      <p></p>
			<p>Predict whether the voltage across resistor 2 is greater than, less than, or equal to the voltage across resistor 1. Explain your answer.</p>
		</investigation>
		
	</subsubsection>
	
<!--NO AI-->
</section>

